23:27:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s totally natural to feel a little guarded when a new manager is incoming—especially if you’ve dealt with bossiness before. But the good news is that the very beginning of a working relationship is actually the best time to shape how you work together. You can set boundaries early while still coming across as collaborative and professional. Here’s how to do it without sparking a power struggle. ### 1. Start with curiosity, not assumptions It’s tempting to brace for impact, but walking in expecting bossiness can make you seem defensive. Instead, treat the first conversations as a two-way discovery. Ask questions like: - “How do you prefer to stay updated on projects?” - “What’s your approach to giving feedback?” - “How much autonomy do you usually give people once you trust they’re on track?” These questions signal that you’re thoughtful about the working relationship, and they’ll also surface early red flags if their answers lean heavily on control. ### 2. Set the tone with a “ways of working” chat Proactively ask for a 30-minute one-on-one specifically to align on how you’ll collaborate. Frame it as, “I’d love to make sure we work together smoothly from day one—can we talk about our working styles?” Then share: - Your core hours and when you’re reachable (e.g., “I focus deeply in the mornings, so I might not reply to Slacks instantly until after 10:30”) - How you handle urgent vs. non-urgent requests - Your preferred level of direction (e.g., “I thrive with clear goals and then trust to execute—regular check-ins work well for me, but I get tripped up when someone wants to review every draft.”) By declaring your preferences calmly, you’re setting a boundary without making it a conflict. A good manager will appreciate the clarity. ### 3. Reduce the urge to micromanage by over-communicating early Bossiness often comes from a manager’s anxiety or lack of visibility. You can prevent that by proactively sharing: - A brief weekly plan or priorities every Monday - A short “done, doing, blockers” update before they have to ask - Heads-ups when something might slip When they see you’re on top of things, they’re far less likely to hover. And if they still hover, you can gently say, “It seems like you’re checking in a lot on X—does my weekly summary give you enough? I want to make sure you feel in the loop without me clogging your inbox.” That reframes it as a care for their time. ### 4. Learn to say “no” by offering trade-offs The moment a bossy manager tests boundaries is often when they pile on work or demand immediate responses. A respectful, firm “no” sounds like: - “I can take that on, but then I’ll need to push Y to next week—which would you prioritise?” - “I’m not available after 6pm, but I can make this my first priority tomorrow morning.” - “I’m not comfortable with that approach because [reason]—could we try [alternative] instead?” You’re not just refusing; you’re showing you care about the outcome while protecting your limits. ### 5. Document agreements and boundaries gently After your early conversations, send a short recap email: “Thanks for the chat—just to confirm, I’ll send a Friday status update instead of daily stand-ups, and I’ll raise any blockers immediately. Let me know if I missed anything.” This creates a written record. If they later insist on daily check-ins, you can refer back: “I’ve been sending the Friday update we agreed on—has something changed that needs more frequent touchpoints?” It’s friendly but holds the line. ### 6. Watch for patterns, not one-off moments Everyone has a bad day. If they get a bit directive once, let it slide. But if you notice consistent attempts to ignore your agreed boundaries, address it calmly: “I’ve noticed you often message me late in the evening expecting a reply—like we discussed, I’m not available after hours unless it’s an emergency. How can we make sure urgent things get flagged properly?” Keep it about the process, not their personality. ### 7. Know your non-negotiables and have a Plan B Some managers simply won’t respect boundaries no matter what. Decide privately what you absolutely won’t tolerate (e.g., shouting, credit-stealing, constant weekend work). If those are crossed repeatedly after you’ve tried the steps above, start documenting for a conversation with HR or your skip-level manager, or quietly explore internal transfers. Boundaries only matter if you’re willing to enforce them. Remember: setting boundaries isn’t about being difficult—it’s about creating a professional, sustainable way of working. A truly bossy manager might resist, but a decent one (even if they have a strong personality) will respect you more for being clear and competent. And if they don’t, you’ve just saved yourself months of frustration by learning that early. Good luck! You’ve got this.
